```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  fig.width = 7,
  fig.height = 4.5,
  fig.path = "stanli-"
)
set.seed(42)
```

_EpiNow2_ fits its models through a choice of Stan backend.
The `rstan` backend uses models compiled when the package is installed and the `cmdstanr` backend compiles a model the first time it is used.
The `stanli` backend uses [stanli](https://github.com/seantalts/stanli), which interprets a Stan program as a graph of operations over precompiled `stan-math` kernels rather than translating it to C++.
Fitting therefore needs no C++ toolchain and preparing a model takes milliseconds.

This vignette shows how to use the backend and compares it against the other two.

# Installation

`stanli` is not on CRAN.
Install the package and then download its runtime library, which is cached after the first call.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
install.packages("stanli", repos = "https://seantalts.r-universe.dev")
stanli::stanli_install()
```

```{r setup}
library("EpiNow2")
library("ggplot2")
library("data.table")
```

# Using the backend

Select the backend through `stan_opts()`.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
estimate_infections(
  data = example_confirmed[1:60],
  generation_time = gt_opts(example_generation_time),
  delays = delay_opts(example_incubation_period + example_reporting_delay),
  stan = stan_opts(backend = "stanli", cores = 4)
)
```

`stanli` runs its chains in parallel within a single call, so `cores` sets how many run at once.
This is the convention the `cmdstanr` backend already uses.

# Comparing the backends

The rest of this vignette fits the same model with each backend and compares the time taken and the estimates produced.

```{r shared-settings}
reported_cases <- example_confirmed[1:60]
generation_time <- gt_opts(example_generation_time)
delays <- delay_opts(example_incubation_period + example_reporting_delay)
backends <- c("rstan", "cmdstanr", "stanli")
```

## Preparing the model

The first cost a user meets is getting a model ready to sample.
The `rstan` model was compiled when the package was installed, so it is already available.

```{r model-prep}
prep <- data.table(
  backend = c("cmdstanr", "stanli"),
  elapsed = c(
    system.time(epinow2_cmdstan_model("estimate_infections"))[["elapsed"]],
    system.time(epinow2_stanli_model("estimate_infections"))[["elapsed"]]
  )
)
prep[]
```

The `cmdstanr` figure is the cost of a cold compile, and later calls reuse the cached binary.
The `stanli` figure is the cost every time.

## Fitting

```{r fit-backends}
fit_with <- function(backend) {
  timing <- system.time(
    fit <- suppressMessages(estimate_infections(
      data = reported_cases,
      generation_time = generation_time,
      delays = delays,
      stan = stan_opts(
        backend = backend, chains = 4, cores = 4,
        samples = 1000, warmup = 500, seed = 20240101
      ),
      verbose = FALSE
    ))
  )
  list(fit = fit, elapsed = timing[["elapsed"]])
}

results <- lapply(backends, fit_with)
names(results) <- backends
```

```{r timings}
timings <- data.table(
  backend = backends,
  elapsed = vapply(results, function(x) x$elapsed, numeric(1))
)
timings[, relative := elapsed / min(elapsed)][]
```

## Comparing estimates

Each backend samples from the same posterior, so the estimates should agree up to Monte Carlo error.

```{r extract-rt}
rt <- rbindlist(lapply(backends, function(backend) {
  out <- as.data.table(
    summary(results[[backend]]$fit, type = "parameters", params = "R")
  )
  out[, backend := backend][]
}))
```

```{r plot-rt}
ggplot(rt, aes(x = date, colour = backend, fill = backend)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower_90, ymax = upper_90), alpha = 0.15,
              colour = NA) +
  geom_line(aes(y = median), linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey40") +
  scale_colour_brewer(palette = "Dark2") +
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "Dark2") +
  labs(x = "Date", y = "Effective reproduction number",
       subtitle = "Median and 90% credible interval") +
  theme_bw() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

The three sets of estimates lie on top of one another.
Comparing the medians against the `rstan` fit puts a number on what remains.

```{r rt-differences}
wide <- dcast(rt, date ~ backend, value.var = "median")
wide[, .(
  cmdstanr = max(abs(cmdstanr - rstan)),
  stanli = max(abs(stanli - rstan))
)]
```

The scalar parameters are easier to read from a table than a plot.

```{r compare-params}
params <- rbindlist(lapply(backends, function(backend) {
  out <- as.data.table(summary(results[[backend]]$fit, type = "parameters"))
  out[is.na(date), .(variable, median)][, backend := backend][]
}))

dcast(params, variable ~ backend, value.var = "median")
```

# Limitations

The backend supports `method = "sampling"` only.
Use the `cmdstanr` backend for variational inference, the Laplace approximation or pathfinder.

It does not support running chains through `{future}`, because it runs them in parallel itself.

`stanli` asks for a starting value for every declared parameter, including zero-size ones that cannot be given a value.
The initial conditions _EpiNow2_ generates therefore cannot be used with it, and random initialisation is used instead.

# Why not stanr

The [stanr](https://github.com/andrjohns/stanr) package offers `stanli` as one of its model backends, alongside a compiled backend and a choice of samplers that includes WALNUTS.
Routing this backend through `stanr` would be tidier, since `stanr` resolves `#include` itself and would bring the extra samplers with it.

It is not usable here yet.
`stanr` lowers a model to its intermediate representation by running the Stan compiler's `O1` optimisation pass in JavaScript, and on models the size of the ones in this package that pass is extremely slow.
Preparing a model took between 26 minutes and 5 hours across the package's models, against milliseconds when `stanli` is driven directly.
The same optimisation runs in `stanli`'s own compiler in well under a second.

This is worth revisiting once that pass is faster, because the `stanr` route would remove the include handling and the initialisation workaround described above.
